{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CggZYSdhStTEDTr8uMxY3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Result: Dq ≈ 433 cm⁻¹ and B ≈ 878 cm⁻¹** (C is not determined by these bands).\n\n**Approach.** A tetrahedral d⁷ ion has the same term pattern as an octahedral d³ ion, with the ground term ⁴A₂ and the spin-allowed bands ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₂, ⁴T₁(F) and ⁴T₁(P). I therefore fitted the three bands with the d³ (octahedral) model, which gives the tetrahedral Dq directly. The octahedral d⁷ fit does not apply here: it returned a poor match (RMSE ≈ 475 cm⁻¹).\n\n**Fit (d³ model, three bands, two free parameters):**\n- 4 330 cm⁻¹ → ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₂ (this is 10Dq, so Dq ≈ 433 cm⁻¹)\n- 7 520 cm⁻¹ → ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₁(F)\n- 18 640 cm⁻¹ → ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₁(P)\n\nThe fit reproduces all three bands to within about 1 cm⁻¹ (residuals +1.1, −0.7 and +0.05 cm⁻¹), so Dq and B are well determined.\n\n**Caveats:**\n- B ≈ 878 cm⁻¹ is about 0.78 of the free-ion value for Co²⁺ (≈ 1 120 cm⁻¹), which is a reasonable nephelauxetic reduction for a tetrahedral Co(II) complex. I did not run the nephelauxetic tool, so this comparison uses the free-ion value from memory.\n- The tool reported C ≈ 4 133 cm⁻¹, but it is an assumed default that these bands do not constrain. I have not reported it as a result.\n\n{\"Dq\": 433, \"B\": 878, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Fitted to the tetrahedral d7 (Td ≅ Oh d3) band pattern with three bands at 4330, 7520 and 18640 cm-1; Dq and B are determined to about 1 cm-1 residual, while C is not constrained by these bands.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4598,"cache_read_input_tokens":7974,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4598,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_